efinition. This is usually a list of numbers or labels, typically in ~~
some unit of time (days, weeks, months, etc.).  Use the “Dynamic()” f~~
unction in your variables to perform dynamic simulation.Typechecking := 1Checking := 1Showhier := 1Saveoptions := 2Savevalues := 0Attribute ReferenceAttribute Date_boughAttribute TheAskattribute Check,Variable,YesModel Loan_policy_analysisTitle: Loan policy analysisDescription: A financial institution that grants loans would like to s~~
elect the interest rate it charges and the credit score approval thre~~
shold for granting the loans so as to maximize net present value to t~~
he institution.~
~
The interest rate selected influences the market demand, since there ~~
is a certain price elasticity in the market.  (I.e., if they set it t~~
oo high, customers will go elsewhere).  If they are too lenient with ~~
the approval threshold, their profits may suffer from a higher defaul~~
t rate down the road.~
~
In addition, the default rate is influenced by the economy -- hence t~~
here is a simplified economic model.  As times get worse, greater def~~
ault rates can be expected.  (Note: I have left it optimizing over th~~
e Mid of Net_present_value, rather than the mean, but you can change ~~
that easily enough, just be ready for increased computation time).~
~
I set up two versions of the optimization.  The parametric optimizati~~
on is a brute-force optimization over all possible values, and takes ~~
quite a while to complete.  Also, since it is just searching a discre~~
te space, it is only approximate.   The full-optimization uses the Fr~~
ontline solver, but requires Analytica Optimizer.  It gets a slightly~~
 better solution and is very fast in comparison.Author: Lonnie Chrisman, Ph.D.~
